-- #822, REWRITTEN for declared kinds (EFFECTS-SEMANTICS.md §6.1/§6.3): the
-- original shape here fed an ABSTRACT higher-kinded parameter `f` a row via
-- `f e Int` on a plain `data` head, with no interface anywhere in the file.
-- That relied on `dataParamKindsRef`'s retired transitive inference; under
-- declared kinds it is no longer just unannotated, it is UNWRITABLE: a `data`
-- head parameter's arrow kind may not contain `Effect` at all -- only an
-- `interface` head may declare `Effect -> Type -> Type` (§6.1's own
-- rationale: only an interface parameter is a type CONSTRUCTOR abstracted
-- over `f e a`, not `f`'s row PLUS its own row). This now pins that a `data`
-- head cannot even express what the old inferred-abstract-head shape needed.
data D (e : Effect) (f : Effect -> Type -> Type) = MkD (Unit -> <e> Unit) (f e Int)

getInner : D e f -> f e Int
getInner (MkD _ x) = x
